    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is one of the most common causes of cancer death and it is becoming increasingly more prevalent. It is a particularly aggressive cancer with almost no effective therapeutic solutions. Activation of the Myc oncogene and the loss of p53 are two of the most common genetic drivers of human HCC.  The cell cycle regulator p53 is a common tumor suppressor which controls cell cycle arrest, DNA repair and apoptosis. More specifically, p53 transactivates its downstream target gene p21, a cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor, that is able to arrest the cells in G1 phase, where they regress to G0 phase. This study utilized a mouse model of HCC in which the tetracycline regulatory system conditionally regulates the restricted concomitant overexpression of the Myc transgene and p21 (Cdkn1a) gene in hepatocytes. Tumor growth and overall survival were measured in the Myc/p21 model to quantify the impact of p21 overexpression.  Immunostaining assays and Western blot were utilized to evaluate the gene expression of various available senescence markers such as p15, p16 and B-galactosidase. This study tests the hypothesis that concomitant induction of a growth promoting oncogene (Myc) and senescence causing cell-cycle inhibitor (p21) will result in a dominant negative tumor regression by the cell-cycle inhibitor p21. The results of this study support the hypothesis that co-expression of p21 and Myc in this mouse model delays tumor growth as a result of p21-induced senescence as characterized by a senescent associated secretory phenotype.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
